# Tana Helper - Cursor Rules

## Code Style & Quality
- Use Ruff for Python formatting (88 char line length, Python 3.11+ features)
- Use Prettier + ESLint for TypeScript (100 char, single quotes, trailing commas)
- Always make minimal, surgical changes - avoid large refactors
- NEVER delete commented code without explicit permission
- Always add comments to explain complex logic and business rules
- Write proper function documentation for all public APIs
- Fix linting errors immediately - user prefers clean code without warnings
- Use `|` union syntax over `Union` in Python type hints
- Use Google-style docstrings for Python functions and classes

## Package Management
- ALWAYS use `uv` commands for Python (`uv run`, `uv add`, etc.)
- ALWAYS use `pnpm` for Node.js (never npm or yarn)
- Use `pyproject.toml` [project.scripts] for Python commands (not Makefiles)

## Architecture Patterns
- Backend: Organize features into separate routers in `/endpoints/`
- Middleware order: Settings refresh → logging → CORS
- Use FastAPI dependency injection with Depends()
- Use Pydantic models for all API interfaces
- Frontend: Use Zustand for global state, React state for local
- Keep React components focused and single-purpose
- Separate logic (hooks) from presentation (components)
- Use axios for API calls with consistent loading/error handling

## Technology-Specific Rules
- FastAPI: Use HTTPException with proper status codes, prefer async for I/O
- React: Use React Router with `/ui` base path, Tailwind + Radix UI for styling
- OpenAI: Always handle rate limits and timeouts gracefully
- Vector DBs: Abstract behind service layer, log operations for debugging
- Use Jinja2 templates for maintainable AI prompts
- Implement exponential backoff for AI API failures

## Security & Configuration
- Always validate inputs with Pydantic models
- Use header-based auth for API tokens (supports runtime override)
- Support both .env files and header overrides for configuration
- Never commit API keys or tokens
- Keep CORS origins restrictive but functional
- Don't leak sensitive information in error responses

## Cross-Platform Considerations
- Use `os.path.join()` for file paths
- macOS calendar features should degrade gracefully on other platforms
- Ensure PyInstaller builds work on all target platforms
- Consider Windows vs Unix process handling differences

## Testing & Debugging
- Use pytest for backend, React Testing Library for frontend
- Test both success and error paths for APIs
- Mock external services (OpenAI, Tana API, vector databases)
- Use Rich logging with structured output and request ID tracking
- Follow snowflake IDs through request lifecycle for debugging
- Use FastAPI's `/docs` for API testing
- Monitor vector database memory consumption

## Performance Guidelines
- Use async/await for I/O bound operations
- Reuse HTTP clients and database connections
- Cache expensive operations (vector embeddings)
- Use React.memo, useMemo, useCallback judiciously
- Implement virtualization for large lists/graphs
- Use dynamic imports for code splitting large components

## Common Issues to Watch For
- CORS problems: Check middleware origins setup
- Static file serving: Verify mount paths match Vite build output
- Environment variables: Check both .env files and header overrides
- Vector DB connections: Verify service availability and configuration
- Request/response model validation in FastAPI endpoints

## Documentation Requirements
- Keep README installation instructions current
- Use FastAPI's automatic OpenAPI generation
- Document React component props and usage patterns
- Provide webhook configuration examples
- Document all environment variables and settings 